- Image generation usage.
- Hosting, database, storage, bandwidth, scheduler, and notification costs.

Where current access, quotas, prices, or availability cannot be verified, label them as unconfirmed and identify the official documentation or account setting that must be checked.

### Required controls

- Default to one video-generation job per production day.
- Default generation concurrency to one.
- Allow an administrator to configure a maximum number of generation jobs per day.
- Provide configurable daily and monthly usage or spending limits wherever supported.
- Provide warning thresholds and a clear usage dashboard.
- Stop automatically when a configured hard limit is reached.
- Do not bypass limits by switching models, accounts, or services without approval.
- Do not silently fall back to a paid model or provider.
- Do not regenerate a video merely because a browser refreshes, a scheduled job runs twice, or a network request times out.
- Check for existing completed assets and active jobs before creating a new generation job.
- Preserve previous generated assets when regenerating.
- Default to one generation attempt. Require administrator action for further attempts unless a safe retry policy has been explicitly approved.
- Display the expected cost before a chargeable action when reliable pricing information is available.
- Record actual charges only when confirmed by the relevant provider; otherwise label figures as estimates.
- Never claim that an estimate is a confirmed bill.

If a generation request times out, determine whether the provider accepted or completed the request before retrying. If the outcome cannot be established, mark the job as requiring reconciliation and request administrator review.

## 6. Persistent Scheduling and Reliable Job Processing

The system must operate independently of the browser session.

Use a supported backend scheduler, scheduled function, or equivalent persistent service. Browser timers, an open tab, local-only scripts, or the administrator's computer must not be required for normal scheduled production.

Before implementation, verify that the selected hosting environment supports the required scheduling frequency, execution duration, storage, authentication, and background processing.

If the existing hosting platform cannot support the workflow reliably, explain the limitation and propose the simplest suitable alternative, including its costs and setup requirements, before implementation.

### Reliability requirements

- Store scheduled jobs and their states persistently.
- Use unique job identifiers and database-backed locking or equivalent concurrency controls.
- Prevent duplicate jobs from repeated clicks, scheduler retries, service restarts, or concurrent requests.
- Persist each job's current stage, attempt count, timestamps, and error details.
- Detect missed or overdue jobs.
- Recover safely after a restart or temporary outage.
- Do not replay a completed operation automatically.
- Reconcile uncertain generation and publishing outcomes before retrying.
- Require administrator review when safe recovery cannot be guaranteed.
- Keep production jobs separate from publishing jobs.
- Provide a visible queue, next-run time, last-run result, and failure details.

A restart must not reset approval status, bypass usage limits, lose job history, or create duplicate external posts.

